# PromptLine Product Suite — Security Hardening Plan (Revised)

A comprehensive application-level security plan across all four PromptLine products:
**Secure** (customer dashboard), **Admin** (internal ops), **Answers Now** (marketing site), and **API** (Fastify backend).

> [!NOTE]
> **Database-level security** (RLS policies, auth hooks, Postgres functions) is **excluded** from this plan — that will be handled separately with your manager.

---

## Deployment Architecture

| Layer | Sandbox (Testing) | Production |
|-------|-------------------|------------|
| **Frontends** | Vercel (preview/sandbox URLs) | Vercel (production URLs) |
| **API Server** | Vercel serverless (sandbox) | Vercel serverless (production) |
| **Edge Functions** | Sandbox Supabase project | Production Supabase project |
| **Database** | Sandbox Supabase project | Production Supabase project |
| **Env Variables** | Separate per Vercel env | Separate per Vercel env |

Key implication: Security changes need to work correctly across **both** deployment environments. CORS allowlists, CSP headers, and rate-limiting configs must account for sandbox + production domains.

---

## Executive Summary

After auditing the codebase, I've identified **6 critical**, **6 high**, and **8 medium** priority application-level security issues:

1. **Every edge function uses `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: '*'`** — any website can call your Supabase functions
2. **Zero rate limiting** on the API server or edge functions
3. **No Content Security Policy** (CSP) or security headers on any frontend
4. **API server (Fastify) has no authentication middleware** — webhook routes accept requests without signature verification
5. **Roles cached in localStorage** on Secure — tamperable via XSS (server RLS mitigates, but it's still a client-side gap)
6. **`.env.example` had real secrets committed historically** (noted in the file itself, lines 67-69)

